\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For CM-type abelian varieties, the Lefschetz-class property that all Tate classes on all powers of a reduction, respectively all Hodge classes on all powers over the complex numbers, are generated by degree-one classes is a sufficient condition for the Tate, respectively Hodge, conjecture.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2013/32}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For CM-type abelian varieties, the Lefschetz-class property that all Tate classes on all powers of a reduction, respectively all Hodge classes on all powers over the complex numbers, are generated by degree-one classes is a sufficient condition for the Tate, respectively Hodge, conjecture.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30002363 (OWR-12495-003). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2013/32}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30002363}

